{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bootstrapping a sandbox (cloning the repo, installing dependencies, running\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setup"},{"type":"text","value":") is the expensive part of a run. The lifecycle config lets you pay that\ncost once and reuse the result: keep one sandbox per thread, snapshot it after\nsetup, and resume instead of re-bootstrapping on the next run."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you must also recover files after a sandbox is gone, configure\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./portable-snapshots-configure","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep Files After Reload"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Provider-native\nsnapshots make bootstrap faster. Portable snapshots save the completed\nworkspace as durable application data."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/app' }),\n    setup: ['corepack enable', 'pnpm install'],\n  }),\n  lifecycle: {\n    reuse: 'thread',          // one sandbox per threadId ('none' = fresh per run)\n    snapshot: 'after-setup',  // snapshot once bootstrapped (provider-permitting)\n    keepAlive: '30m',         // hint to keep the sandbox warm between runs\n    destroyOnComplete: false, // keep it for the next run\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-lifecycle-object","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The lifecycle object"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Field"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What it controls"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reuse"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'thread'"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps one sandbox per "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'none'"},{"type":"text","value":" provisions a fresh sandbox per run."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshot"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'after-setup'"},{"type":"text","value":" snapshots the workspace once bootstrap finishes, on snapshot-capable providers."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keepAlive"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Duration hint (e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'30m'"},{"type":"text","value":") for how long the sandbox should stay warm between runs. Nothing in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" reads it today; it is a hint for providers and host apps. Provider idle timers live on the "},{"type":"link","href":"./providers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"provider"}]},{"type":"text","value":" config."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destroyOnComplete"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":", the sandbox survives the run so the next one can resume it."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshotMaxAge"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Duration (e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'24h'"},{"type":"text","value":") after which a stored snapshot is treated as stale and re-created."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"these-fields-govern-completion-not-cancellation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These fields govern "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completion"}]},{"type":"text","value":", not cancellation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keepAlive"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destroyOnComplete"},{"type":"text","value":" describe what happens when a run "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"finishes"}]},{"type":"text","value":".\nThey never keep a sandbox alive through an abort: closing the agent's IO stream\ndoes not kill the agent process, so an explicit cancel destroys the sandbox\nregardless of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destroyOnComplete"},{"type":"text","value":", and that is the only reliable way to stop the\nagent burning tokens."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"disconnect"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is the third case, and it is not the same as a cancel. A user\npressing Stop and a user closing the tab produce an identical connection close.\nOn a run with durability wired, a disconnect neither completes nor destroys: the\nsandbox stays up, the agent keeps working, and the run record is marked detached\nso a later request can take it over. With no durability wired, a disconnect\ndestroys, exactly as an abort does. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./takeover#detach-vs-cancel","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Takeover & Detached Runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"snapshot-after-setup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Snapshot after setup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When the provider supports snapshots (see "},{"type":"link","href":"./providers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Providers"}]},{"type":"text","value":"), bootstrap\nautomatically takes a snapshot after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setup"},{"type":"text","value":" completes. The snapshot caches the\nfully bootstrapped "},{"type":"link","href":"./workspace","children":[{"type":"text","value":"workspace"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (the cloned repo with dependencies\ninstalled) so subsequent runs resume from it instead of re-running the setup\nsteps, which dramatically reduces cold-start time."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshot: 'after-setup'"},{"type":"text","value":" is the default whenever the provider reports snapshot\nsupport, so you usually do not set it explicitly. Providers without snapshot\nsupport (e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localProcessSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":") skip the snapshot step silently."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/app' }),\n    setup: ['corepack enable', 'pnpm install'],\n  }),\n  lifecycle: {\n    reuse: 'thread',\n    // 'after-setup' is the default when the provider supports snapshots.\n    snapshot: 'after-setup',\n    // Optional: re-create (re-bootstrap) when the snapshot is older than this.\n    snapshotMaxAge: '24h',\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"stale-snapshots","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stale snapshots"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshotMaxAge"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts a duration string ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'24h'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'30m'"},{"type":"text","value":", etc.). When the\nstored snapshot is older than the limit, the sandbox treats it as stale and\nre-creates from scratch, re-running setup and capturing a new snapshot. Leave\nit unset to keep snapshots indefinitely."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-sandbox-instance-key","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The sandbox instance key"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A sandbox is keyed by a compound "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxInstanceKey"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"sandboxInstanceKey = hash(threadId + sandbox.id + provider + workspaceHash + tenant?)","lang":"txt"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because the key folds in the workspace hash, the provider, and an optional\ntenant. Changing any input that would invalidate a cached environment means the\nrepo, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setup"},{"type":"text","value":" steps, the provider image, or the tenant, produces a\ndifferent key. That means you safely start a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fresh"}]},{"type":"text","value":" sandbox rather than\nresuming a stale one whose snapshot no longer matches your config. Keep those\ninputs stable across runs to keep hitting the same warm sandbox."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"ensure-order","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ensure order"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a run needs a sandbox, the layer resolves it in this order:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resume the running sandbox"}]},{"type":"text","value":", if a live sandbox already exists for the key."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Restore the latest snapshot"}]},{"type":"text","value":", recreate from the most recent snapshot,\nskipping setup."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create fresh and bootstrap"}]},{"type":"text","value":", clone, install, run "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setup"},{"type":"text","value":", then snapshot."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each step falls through to the next only when the prior one is unavailable. This\nis what turns a warm thread into a near-instant start, and a cold one into a\nfull bootstrap."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Portable sandbox snapshots run after this lifecycle work. They restore a saved\nworkspace only into a newly created private sandbox. They never overwrite a\nlive resumed sandbox. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./portable-snapshots","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Portable Snapshots"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which providers support durable disk, snapshots, and resume-by-id is listed on\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./providers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Providers"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Providers without durable disk or snapshots (e.g. ephemeral containers)\nre-create and re-bootstrap under the same identity: the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxInstanceKey"},{"type":"text","value":"\nstays stable, but every run pays the bootstrap cost because there is nothing\ndurable to resume."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"the-lifecycle-object","text":"The lifecycle object","level":2},{"id":"these-fields-govern-completion-not-cancellation","text":"These fields govern completion, not cancellation","level":3},{"id":"snapshot-after-setup","text":"Snapshot after setup","level":2},{"id":"stale-snapshots","text":"Stale snapshots","level":3},{"id":"the-sandbox-instance-key","text":"The sandbox instance key","level":2},{"id":"ensure-order","text":"Ensure order","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nBootstrapping a sandbox (cloning the repo, installing dependencies, running\n`setup`) is the expensive part of a run. The lifecycle config lets you pay that\ncost once and reuse the result: keep one sandbox per thread, snapshot it after\nsetup, and resume instead of re-bootstrapping on the next run.\n\nWhen you must also recover files after a sandbox is gone, configure\n[Keep Files After Reload](./portable-snapshots-configure). Provider-native\nsnapshots make bootstrap faster. Portable snapshots save the completed\nworkspace as durable application data.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/app' }),\n    setup: ['corepack enable', 'pnpm install'],\n  }),\n  lifecycle: {\n    reuse: 'thread',          // one sandbox per threadId ('none' = fresh per run)\n    snapshot: 'after-setup',  // snapshot once bootstrapped (provider-permitting)\n    keepAlive: '30m',         // hint to keep the sandbox warm between runs\n    destroyOnComplete: false, // keep it for the next run\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## The lifecycle object\n\n| Field               | What it controls                                                                            |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `reuse`             | `'thread'` keeps one sandbox per `threadId`; `'none'` provisions a fresh sandbox per run.    |\n| `snapshot`          | `'after-setup'` snapshots the workspace once bootstrap finishes, on snapshot-capable providers. |\n| `keepAlive`         | Duration hint (e.g. `'30m'`) for how long the sandbox should stay warm between runs. Nothing in `@tanstack/ai-sandbox` reads it today; it is a hint for providers and host apps. Provider idle timers live on the [provider](./providers) config. |\n| `destroyOnComplete` | When `false`, the sandbox survives the run so the next one can resume it.                    |\n| `snapshotMaxAge`    | Duration (e.g. `'24h'`) after which a stored snapshot is treated as stale and re-created.   |\n\n### These fields govern *completion*, not cancellation\n\n`keepAlive` and `destroyOnComplete` describe what happens when a run **finishes**.\nThey never keep a sandbox alive through an abort: closing the agent's IO stream\ndoes not kill the agent process, so an explicit cancel destroys the sandbox\nregardless of `destroyOnComplete`, and that is the only reliable way to stop the\nagent burning tokens.\n\nA **disconnect** is the third case, and it is not the same as a cancel. A user\npressing Stop and a user closing the tab produce an identical connection close.\nOn a run with durability wired, a disconnect neither completes nor destroys: the\nsandbox stays up, the agent keeps working, and the run record is marked detached\nso a later request can take it over. With no durability wired, a disconnect\ndestroys, exactly as an abort does. See\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](./takeover#detach-vs-cancel).\n\n## Snapshot after setup\n\nWhen the provider supports snapshots (see [Providers](./providers)), bootstrap\nautomatically takes a snapshot after `setup` completes. The snapshot caches the\nfully bootstrapped [workspace](./workspace) (the cloned repo with dependencies\ninstalled) so subsequent runs resume from it instead of re-running the setup\nsteps, which dramatically reduces cold-start time.\n\n`snapshot: 'after-setup'` is the default whenever the provider reports snapshot\nsupport, so you usually do not set it explicitly. Providers without snapshot\nsupport (e.g. `localProcessSandbox`) skip the snapshot step silently.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/app' }),\n    setup: ['corepack enable', 'pnpm install'],\n  }),\n  lifecycle: {\n    reuse: 'thread',\n    // 'after-setup' is the default when the provider supports snapshots.\n    snapshot: 'after-setup',\n    // Optional: re-create (re-bootstrap) when the snapshot is older than this.\n    snapshotMaxAge: '24h',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Stale snapshots\n\n`snapshotMaxAge` accepts a duration string (`'24h'`, `'30m'`, etc.). When the\nstored snapshot is older than the limit, the sandbox treats it as stale and\nre-creates from scratch, re-running setup and capturing a new snapshot. Leave\nit unset to keep snapshots indefinitely.\n\n## The sandbox instance key\n\nA sandbox is keyed by a compound `sandboxInstanceKey`:\n\n```txt\nsandboxInstanceKey = hash(threadId + sandbox.id + provider + workspaceHash + tenant?)\n```\n\nBecause the key folds in the workspace hash, the provider, and an optional\ntenant. Changing any input that would invalidate a cached environment means the\nrepo, the `setup` steps, the provider image, or the tenant, produces a\ndifferent key. That means you safely start a **fresh** sandbox rather than\nresuming a stale one whose snapshot no longer matches your config. Keep those\ninputs stable across runs to keep hitting the same warm sandbox.\n\n## Ensure order\n\nWhen a run needs a sandbox, the layer resolves it in this order:\n\n1. **Resume the running sandbox**, if a live sandbox already exists for the key.\n2. **Restore the latest snapshot**, recreate from the most recent snapshot,\n   skipping setup.\n3. **Create fresh and bootstrap**, clone, install, run `setup`, then snapshot.\n\nEach step falls through to the next only when the prior one is unavailable. This\nis what turns a warm thread into a near-instant start, and a cold one into a\nfull bootstrap.\n\nPortable sandbox snapshots run after this lifecycle work. They restore a saved\nworkspace only into a newly created private sandbox. They never overwrite a\nlive resumed sandbox. See [Portable Snapshots](./portable-snapshots).\n\n> Which providers support durable disk, snapshots, and resume-by-id is listed on\n> [Providers](./providers).\n\nProviders without durable disk or snapshots (e.g. ephemeral containers)\nre-create and re-bootstrap under the same identity: the `sandboxInstanceKey`\nstays stable, but every run pays the bootstrap cost because there is nothing\ndurable to resume.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Lifecycle & Snapshots\nid: lifecycle\norder: 8\ndescription: \"Control sandbox reuse, snapshot the bootstrapped workspace after setup, and resume across runs to cut cold-start cost.\"\n---\n\nBootstrapping a sandbox (cloning the repo, installing dependencies, running\n`setup`) is the expensive part of a run. The lifecycle config lets you pay that\ncost once and reuse the result: keep one sandbox per thread, snapshot it after\nsetup, and resume instead of re-bootstrapping on the next run.\n\nWhen you must also recover files after a sandbox is gone, configure\n[Keep Files After Reload](./portable-snapshots-configure). Provider-native\nsnapshots make bootstrap faster. Portable snapshots save the completed\nworkspace as durable application data.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/app' }),\n    setup: ['corepack enable', 'pnpm install'],\n  }),\n  lifecycle: {\n    reuse: 'thread',          // one sandbox per threadId ('none' = fresh per run)\n    snapshot: 'after-setup',  // snapshot once bootstrapped (provider-permitting)\n    keepAlive: '30m',         // hint to keep the sandbox warm between runs\n    destroyOnComplete: false, // keep it for the next run\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## The lifecycle object\n\n| Field               | What it controls                                                                            |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `reuse`             | `'thread'` keeps one sandbox per `threadId`; `'none'` provisions a fresh sandbox per run.    |\n| `snapshot`          | `'after-setup'` snapshots the workspace once bootstrap finishes, on snapshot-capable providers. |\n| `keepAlive`         | Duration hint (e.g. `'30m'`) for how long the sandbox should stay warm between runs. Nothing in `@tanstack/ai-sandbox` reads it today; it is a hint for providers and host apps. Provider idle timers live on the [provider](./providers) config. |\n| `destroyOnComplete` | When `false`, the sandbox survives the run so the next one can resume it.                    |\n| `snapshotMaxAge`    | Duration (e.g. `'24h'`) after which a stored snapshot is treated as stale and re-created.   |\n\n### These fields govern *completion*, not cancellation\n\n`keepAlive` and `destroyOnComplete` describe what happens when a run **finishes**.\nThey never keep a sandbox alive through an abort: closing the agent's IO stream\ndoes not kill the agent process, so an explicit cancel destroys the sandbox\nregardless of `destroyOnComplete`, and that is the only reliable way to stop the\nagent burning tokens.\n\nA **disconnect** is the third case, and it is not the same as a cancel. A user\npressing Stop and a user closing the tab produce an identical connection close.\nOn a run with durability wired, a disconnect neither completes nor destroys: the\nsandbox stays up, the agent keeps working, and the run record is marked detached\nso a later request can take it over. With no durability wired, a disconnect\ndestroys, exactly as an abort does. See\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](./takeover#detach-vs-cancel).\n\n## Snapshot after setup\n\nWhen the provider supports snapshots (see [Providers](./providers)), bootstrap\nautomatically takes a snapshot after `setup` completes. The snapshot caches the\nfully bootstrapped [workspace](./workspace) (the cloned repo with dependencies\ninstalled) so subsequent runs resume from it instead of re-running the setup\nsteps, which dramatically reduces cold-start time.\n\n`snapshot: 'after-setup'` is the default whenever the provider reports snapshot\nsupport, so you usually do not set it explicitly. Providers without snapshot\nsupport (e.g. `localProcessSandbox`) skip the snapshot step silently.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/app' }),\n    setup: ['corepack enable', 'pnpm install'],\n  }),\n  lifecycle: {\n    reuse: 'thread',\n    // 'after-setup' is the default when the provider supports snapshots.\n    snapshot: 'after-setup',\n    // Optional: re-create (re-bootstrap) when the snapshot is older than this.\n    snapshotMaxAge: '24h',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Stale snapshots\n\n`snapshotMaxAge` accepts a duration string (`'24h'`, `'30m'`, etc.). When the\nstored snapshot is older than the limit, the sandbox treats it as stale and\nre-creates from scratch, re-running setup and capturing a new snapshot. Leave\nit unset to keep snapshots indefinitely.\n\n## The sandbox instance key\n\nA sandbox is keyed by a compound `sandboxInstanceKey`:\n\n```txt\nsandboxInstanceKey = hash(threadId + sandbox.id + provider + workspaceHash + tenant?)\n```\n\nBecause the key folds in the workspace hash, the provider, and an optional\ntenant. Changing any input that would invalidate a cached environment means the\nrepo, the `setup` steps, the provider image, or the tenant, produces a\ndifferent key. That means you safely start a **fresh** sandbox rather than\nresuming a stale one whose snapshot no longer matches your config. Keep those\ninputs stable across runs to keep hitting the same warm sandbox.\n\n## Ensure order\n\nWhen a run needs a sandbox, the layer resolves it in this order:\n\n1. **Resume the running sandbox**, if a live sandbox already exists for the key.\n2. **Restore the latest snapshot**, recreate from the most recent snapshot,\n   skipping setup.\n3. **Create fresh and bootstrap**, clone, install, run `setup`, then snapshot.\n\nEach step falls through to the next only when the prior one is unavailable. This\nis what turns a warm thread into a near-instant start, and a cold one into a\nfull bootstrap.\n\nPortable sandbox snapshots run after this lifecycle work. They restore a saved\nworkspace only into a newly created private sandbox. They never overwrite a\nlive resumed sandbox. See [Portable Snapshots](./portable-snapshots).\n\n> Which providers support durable disk, snapshots, and resume-by-id is listed on\n> [Providers](./providers).\n\nProviders without durable disk or snapshots (e.g. ephemeral containers)\nre-create and re-bootstrap under the same identity: the `sandboxInstanceKey`\nstays stable, but every run pays the bootstrap cost because there is nothing\ndurable to resume.\n"}
